// INST1 Academy question bank, units 4 and 5. All questions are original jobsite scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u4l1
{id:"u4l1-1",t:"u4l1",q:"Forty cables are due off the pallet in the TR this morning, and a cutter is already in your hand. What is the FIRST step before any cable leaves its box?",a:["Cut every cable to the length of the longest run so each one will reach any outlet on the floor","Label each cable with its schedule ID at the box end before it is cut from the box","Strip the jacket ends at the box so the termination crew can start punching down at once","Pull every cable into the ceiling uncut and trim each one at its outlet once it has arrived"],c:1,e:"The label ties each cable to the pull schedule, the outlet, and the test record, and it goes on before the cut. Cutting every cable to one length wastes cable, and trimming at the outlet loses the match to the schedule."},
{id:"u4l1-2",t:"u4l1",q:"A floor needs 30 runs. Each run is 210 ft of path, and the plan gives 10 ft of slack at each end. The cable comes in 1000 ft boxes. How many boxes should you order?",a:["7 boxes","6 boxes","8 boxes","10 boxes"],c:2,e:"Cut length = 210 + 10 + 10 = 230 ft. Runs per box = 1000 / 230 = 4.3, rounded down to 4 whole runs. Boxes = 30 / 4 = 7.5, rounded up to 8. Dividing total footage (6900 ft) by 1000 gives 7 and leaves you short."},
{id:"u4l1-3",t:"u4l1",q:"The pull turns a hard corner at the end of the corridor, and the cable keeps scraping the wall as it goes round. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Hold the cable against the wall at the corner with a helper's gloved hand","Bend a short steel conduit stub tightly and let the cable slide over its curve","Pull faster through the corner so that the cable has less time to scrape the wall","Set a sheave or roller at the corner sized so the cable follows its bend radius"],c:3,e:"A sheave or roller guides the cable around the corner at its minimum bend radius without rubbing. A hand against a wall, extra speed, or a tight steel stub all scrape or over-bend the cable."},
{id:"u4l1-4",t:"u4l1",q:"The pull end is two corners away from the feed end, and nobody at the feed end can see the cable. What is the BEST way to keep the pull safe?",a:["Put people at the feed end, the pull end, and the corners, all on radios, so any one can call a stop","Pull at a steady speed and have the feed end wait for the rope to go slack","Send a runner between the ends every few minutes to report how the pull is going","Pull in short sudden jerks so the feed end can feel each tug through the cable"],c:0,e:"Radios let anyone stop the pull the moment the cable binds, which keeps tension inside the limit. Waiting for slack rope, a runner, or jerky pulls reacts too late and stretches the cable."},
{id:"u4l1-5",t:"u4l1",q:"A boxed cable is lying flat on the floor of the TR, and it twists into kinks as the crew draws it out through the top. What is the BEST correction?",a:["Roll the box onto its side and draw the cable off the end of the box, since a box on its side never twists it","Put the box or reel on a stand or spool tree so it pays off freely","Have a helper stand on the box so it cannot spin while the cable is drawn out through the hole","Cut the cable into short lengths and join them with butt splices after the pull is finished"],c:1,e:"A reel stand or spool tree lets the cable unwind without twisting or kinking. Holding the box still or rolling it does not remove the twist, and splices are not allowed in horizontal copper."},

// u4l2
{id:"u4l2-1",t:"u4l2",q:"A conduit run is long with four bends and has no string in it. What is the BEST way to get a line through?",a:["Push a steel fish tape the whole length from one end until it comes out the far end","Feed a coil of heavy pull rope in from one end and wait for it to reach the other","Blow or vacuum a light line through with a piston or mouse sized to the conduit","Drop a weighted string into the first box and let it fall down through the bends"],c:2,e:"Blown or vacuum line carries a piston and string through long runs with bends. A steel tape jams at the bends, and rope or a weighted string cannot travel through a horizontal conduit by itself."},
{id:"u4l2-2",t:"u4l2",q:"The foreman asks you to fish a steel tape into a wall box that holds power conductors. What is the FIRST thing to do?",a:["Wear rubber gloves and push the tape in slowly with one hand on the box","Wrap the end of the tape in electrical tape and feed it in carefully past the conductors","Switch to a fiberglass tape and keep your hands well clear of the conductors","Have the electrician de-energize and lock out the circuit before any tape goes in, or choose another route"],c:3,e:"Never fish any tape into a box or raceway with energized conductors. The electrician de-energizes and locks out the circuit first. Fiberglass does not conduct, which makes it the better tape near electrical work, but it is not permission to work live, and gloves or tape on the end do not make a steel tape safe."},
{id:"u4l2-3",t:"u4l2",q:"The cable is pulled and the conduit is now empty of line. What should you do before leaving the pathway?",a:["Leave a new pull string in the conduit, tied off at both ends","Leave the old rope tied to the cable at both ends as a spare for the next pull","Leave it as it is and let the next crew fish their own line","Pull the rope out so that the conduit is empty for the inspector"],c:0,e:"A conduit always keeps a new pull string for the next crew, tied off so it cannot slip back inside. An empty conduit forces the next crew to fish a line again."},
{id:"u4l2-4",t:"u4l2",q:"You want the length of a conduit run while you draw a line through it. What is the BEST method?",a:["Count the ceiling tiles along the route and multiply by the width of a tile","Read the footage marks on the line where it comes out of the far end","Scale the run off the floor plan and add 10 percent for the bends","Weigh the line on the reel before and after the run and compare the two"],c:1,e:"A tape-style pull line is printed with footage marks, so reading the mark at the exit gives the real length. The other methods only estimate."},
{id:"u4l2-5",t:"u4l2",q:"You must run a line down inside a hollow wall from the ceiling to an old outlet box. The electrician has de-energized and locked out the power wiring in the cavity. What is the BEST tool?",a:["A steel fish tape pushed down the cavity beside the power wiring","A coil of pull rope dropped from the ceiling opening and left to find the box","A fiberglass push rod or glow rod, which does not conduct","A blown line with a piston, because every wall cavity acts as a conduit"],c:2,e:"A fiberglass or glow rod works in open cavities and does not conduct, which is the safer choice around wiring even after lockout. A steel tape is a hazard if a circuit is missed, dropped rope snags, and a cavity is not a conduit, so a piston has nothing to travel in."},

// u4l3
{id:"u4l3-1",t:"u4l3",q:"Eight 4-pair cables are pulled together as one bundle through a long conduit. What is the most tension the pull may put on the bundle?",a:["25 lbf","110 lbf","350 lbf","200 lbf"],c:3,e:"The limit is 25 lbf per 4-pair cable, multiplied by the number of cables: 8 x 25 = 200 lbf. The single-cable figure of 25 would be far too low, and 110 is a newton value, not pounds."},
{id:"u4l3-2",t:"u4l3",q:"A fiber cable 0.5 in across is being pulled under tension around a corner. What is the smallest radius the cable may be bent to?",a:["10 in","5 in","2 in","20 in"],c:0,e:"Fiber under pulling tension needs 20 x OD: 20 x 0.5 = 10 in. Five inches is the 10 x OD value for no load, and 2 in is the 4 x OD rule for UTP."},
{id:"u4l3-3",t:"u4l3",q:"A multipair backbone cable is 0.75 in across and is being laid round a sweep in a tray. What is its minimum bend radius?",a:["3 in","7.5 in","15 in","1.5 in"],c:1,e:"Multipair copper needs 10 x OD: 10 x 0.75 = 7.5 in. Three inches is 4 x OD, the rule for 4-pair UTP, and 15 in is the 20 x OD figure that applies to fiber under tension."},
{id:"u4l3-4",t:"u4l3",q:"After a pull you secure a bundle of cables to a J-hook with plastic ties cinched as tight as they will go. What is the BEST correction?",a:["Add a second row of tight ties so the load is spread along the bundle","Leave the ties as they are and test the cables to confirm every one still passes","Cut the ties and use hook-and-loop ties left loose enough for the bundle to slide","Replace the ties with a few turns of electrical tape for a smoother surface"],c:2,e:"Cinched ties crush the jacket and distort the pairs, which fails crosstalk and return loss later. Use hook-and-loop ties and leave the bundle loose."},
{id:"u4l3-5",t:"u4l3",q:"Halfway through a pull the rope goes stiff and the tension meter climbs. What should you do FIRST?",a:["Add more people to the rope and keep a steady pull until the cable breaks free","Pour more lubricant from the truck at the conduit mouth and pull harder","Switch to a faster winch speed so the cable does not stay in the stiff section","Stop the pull and find the cause of the extra tension before going on"],c:3,e:"Stiffness means something is wrong: a snag, a missing sheave, too many cables, or a kink. Pulling harder only raises the tension past the limit, so stop and find the cause."},

// u4l4
{id:"u4l4-1",t:"u4l4",q:"Eight cables must be pulled together through a conduit with two bends. What is the BEST way to start the bundle?",a:["Make a smooth tapered head with a basket grip or a tape-and-loop head, on a swivel","Twist the eight cable ends together tightly and tie the pull rope round them","Tape the eight ends flat into one square block so that they pass the bends together","Tie each cable to the rope separately with a knot, at spaced intervals"],c:0,e:"A smooth, tapered head on a swivel slides past joints and bushings without catching. A twisted or square head snags, and separate knots load the cables unevenly."},
{id:"u4l4-2",t:"u4l4",q:"A 70 ft conduit run between two boxes has three 90-degree bends. What should you do?",a:["Pull the cable through as drawn since the run is under 30 m (100 ft)","Add a pull box so that no section has more than two 90-degree bends","Remove one bend by cutting the conduit and connecting it with a union","Add a lubricant and use a larger crew to pull through the third bend"],c:1,e:"The limit is two 90-degree bends between pull points, so a third bend needs a pull box, placed so each section stays within the limit. The 30 m length limit is a separate rule and does not excuse the extra bend."},
{id:"u4l4-3",t:"u4l4",q:"At the feed end the cables kink and twist where they enter the conduit mouth. What is the BEST correction?",a:["Feed in short bursts so that the cables have time to straighten between pulls","Feed the cables from a bundle held in a loop on the floor beside the conduit mouth","Feed straight off the stands through a bushing, with a helper holding the cables apart","Hold the cables together in one hand and feed them in with a sharp push"],c:2,e:"Straight feeding off stands with a bushing and a helper to keep the cables from crossing prevents kinks. Loops on the floor and pushed bursts add twist."},
{id:"u4l4-4",t:"u4l4",q:"The pulled cable reaches a pull box midway along the run. What is the BEST way to handle it?",a:["Bend the cable sharply inside the box to turn it toward the conduit for the next section","Leave the cable coiled on the bottom of the box until the next crew arrives to continue","Cut the cable in the box and splice it before the next section","Pull out a loose loop, check it for damage, lay it at its bend radius, and pull on"],c:3,e:"A pull box is a feed and pull point, never a place to bend or splice. A loose loop at the cable's bend radius lets you check the cable and continue the pull."},
{id:"u4l4-5",t:"u4l4",q:"A helper at the feed end grabs the cable jackets and hauls on them hand over hand to speed the pull. What is the BEST response?",a:["Stop him; the rope and grip carry the pull and he only guides the cables","Have him haul only on the top cables of the bundle, where the jackets are easiest to grip by hand","Let him continue, because extra hauling at the feed end lowers the tension at the far end of the pull","Give him pulling gloves with a better grip so he can haul without slipping on the jackets"],c:0,e:"Hauling on the jackets adds uncontrolled tension and can stretch or scrape the cables. The rope through a swivel and grip carries the pull, and feed-end helpers only guide the cables."},

// u4l5
{id:"u4l5-1",t:"u4l5",q:"The foreman asks you to space the J-hooks 2 m (6.6 ft) apart to save hardware in an open ceiling. What is the most spacing you may use?",a:["3 m (10 ft)","1.5 m (5 ft)","2.4 m (8 ft)","0.6 m (2 ft)"],c:1,e:"Noncontinuous supports such as J-hooks may be no more than 1.5 m (5 ft) apart, so 2 m is too wide. Wider spacing lets the bundle sag onto the ceiling and the tile grid."},
{id:"u4l5-2",t:"u4l5",q:"The last crew hung J-hooks from the ceiling grid wires, and you are about to add your cables. What is the BEST action?",a:["Add your cables, since the hooks are already in place and carry the same load","Move the hooks to the tile grid itself where the rails are stiffer than the wires","Remove them and hang hooks on their own supports from the building structure","Keep the hooks and add a second grid wire next to each one to share the load"],c:2,e:"Supports for cable must hang independently from the structure, never from grid wires or the grid. Adding load to wires meant for the ceiling is a violation and a falling hazard."},
{id:"u4l5-3",t:"u4l5",q:"The ceiling above the corridor is a return-air plenum, and the only cable left on the truck is riser-rated. What should you do?",a:["Use the riser cable and keep the run short so the fire risk is limited","Use the riser cable but place it in a plastic raceway through the plenum","Use the riser cable above the first ten feet and plenum cable beyond that","Hold the pull and get plenum-rated cable (CMP) for the air-handling space"],c:3,e:"Plenum space needs plenum-rated cable. A lower rating such as riser may never replace a higher one, whatever the length of the run."},
{id:"u4l5-4",t:"u4l5",q:"Your planned route passes over a row of fluorescent fixtures and next to a sprinkler head. What is the BEST way to handle it?",a:["Route it so it stays at least 130 mm (5.12 in) from the lamps and clear of the sprinkler","Lay the cable on top of the fixtures, which hold it up and keep it off the grid","Move the sprinkler head a short way to open a path for the cable","Wrap the cable in foil where it crosses the fixtures to reduce interference"],c:0,e:"Keep the bundle away from lights, ballasts, sprinklers, and access panels: 130 mm (5.12 in) from fluorescent lamps. Another trade's devices are never moved, and foil is no substitute for distance."},
{id:"u4l5-5",t:"u4l5",q:"A helper drags the cable across the ceiling tile grid to save time. What is the BEST method?",a:["Allow the dragging, but keep the cable well away from the sharp edges of the grid and tile","Lay it hand to hand into the hooks, with a helper at each change of direction","Lay the cable on top of the tiles first and hook it up afterwards once the pull is done","Drag it slowly with one helper at the far end"],c:1,e:"Dragging scrapes the jacket and saws the pairs. Laying the cable into the hooks keeps it off the grid and the tile, and helpers at the turns control the bend."},

// u4l6
{id:"u4l6-1",t:"u4l6",q:"You are about to start a top-down riser pull with a heavy backbone cable. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Feed the end down the shaft and see how fast the cable falls before setting a brake","Lower the end to the basement on a rope and tie it off before setting up the reel","Set the reel on a stand at the top, set the brake, and confirm radio contact at every floor","Cut the cable to length at the top so that the reel can be set aside"],c:2,e:"Gravity does the pulling, so the risk is a runaway. A brake at the reel and radios to every floor come first, before any cable goes down the shaft."},
{id:"u4l6-2",t:"u4l6",q:"The cable end has reached the basement and the whole weight hangs from the top reel. What should be done?",a:["Leave it hanging until the firestop is installed since the jacket will carry it","Tie the cable to the lowest floor opening so the weight rests on the basement","Cut the cable at the top to take the weight off the reel","Support it at each floor with grips or clamps so the weight is shared"],c:3,e:"Each floor takes part of the weight through a split-mesh grip or clamp. Hanging from the jacket or the top reel stretches the pairs, and cutting the cable ruins a continuous riser."},
{id:"u4l6-3",t:"u4l6",q:"During a top-down pull the cable binds in the sleeve at floor 3. What is the BEST response?",a:["Call stop on the radio, hold the brake, and free the cable","Add a second helper at the top to push the cable harder past the snag in the sleeve","Cut the cable above the sleeve and run a new length through from the floor above","Release the brake so the weight of the cable drags it through the sleeve under its own weight"],c:0,e:"Anyone on the radio can call a stop, and the brake holds the cable while it is freed. Releasing the brake, pushing, or cutting the cable risks a runaway or ruins a continuous riser."},
{id:"u4l6-4",t:"u4l6",q:"Both the roof room and the basement are open for a heavy riser cable. The foreman asks where to set the reel. What is the BEST choice?",a:["At the basement, because pulling against gravity keeps the cable from running away","At the roof, so gravity helps and the job is to control the descent with a brake","At the roof, because a top-down pull needs no brake or floor supports","At the basement, because the floor openings can then be firestopped from below only"],c:1,e:"Top down lets gravity do the work, so the task becomes control with a brake and support at each floor. Bottom up fights the weight and needs a winch and tension monitoring."},
{id:"u4l6-5",t:"u4l6",q:"The riser cable is in place and every floor opening has cable through it. What should happen next?",a:["Leave the openings open until the final inspection so the inspector can see the cable","Fill the openings with foam from a can, and label the cable at the top only","Firestop each opening with a listed system and label the cable at every floor","Splice the cable at the middle floor so it can be reterminated later"],c:2,e:"Every floor opening is firestopped with a listed system, and the cable is labeled at every floor. Foam is not a firestop, and a riser is never spliced."},

// u4l7
{id:"u4l7-1",t:"u4l7",q:"The roof room is locked and too small for a reel, and the backbone cable must go up six floors. What is the BEST method?",a:["Pull it up in short lengths and splice them together at each floor","Hand-carry the reel up the stairs and feed the cable down from each landing","Lift the cable up the shaft by hand with a helper at each floor","Pull from the bottom up with a winch at the top, a swivel, and a tension monitor"],c:3,e:"When the top is not available, bottom-up pulling uses a winch with a swivel and tension monitoring against the manufacturer's limit. A riser is never spliced."},
{id:"u4l7-2",t:"u4l7",q:"The tension meter on a bottom-up pull reads right at the manufacturer's limit at the fourth floor, with two floors to go. What should you do?",a:["Stop, find the cause of the high tension, and fix it before continuing","Speed up the winch so that the pull is finished before the cable warms up","Keep a steady pull and finish quickly; two floors will not add much","Add lubricant of any type and continue at the same speed"],c:0,e:"Tension climbs with every floor, and a reading near the limit means the pull must stop until the cause is found. Never exceed the rating to finish the pull."},
{id:"u4l7-3",t:"u4l7",q:"A bottom-up pull has just brought the end to the top floor. What must happen before the winch releases the tension?",a:["The end is tied to the winch cable and left to hold the whole weight of the riser","The cable is secured at each floor so every support holds it","The cable is cut at the top so the tension drops to zero before the winch lets off","The cable is clamped at the top floor only, since one clamp can hold the full weight"],c:1,e:"The cable must be supported at each floor before the tension is released. Releasing first, or relying on the pulling eye or a single clamp, puts the whole weight on one point."},
{id:"u4l7-4",t:"u4l7",q:"On a bottom-up pull the pull line is joined straight to the pulling eye, and the cable spins as it rises. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Clamp a steel plate across the joint so that the eye cannot turn on the line","Slow the winch to a crawl until the spinning stops by itself on the way up","Fit a swivel between the line and the eye to keep twist out of the cable","Add a second pull line beside the first to share the load"],c:2,e:"A swivel lets the pull line rotate without twisting the cable. A plate, a slower winch, or a second line does not stop twist from building up."},
{id:"u4l7-5",t:"u4l7",q:"The winch operator asks which tension limit to watch during the pull of a large riser cable. What is the correct answer?",a:["The rated pull of the winch itself, since the winch sets how hard the cable can be drawn","The weight of the reel in pounds","25 lbf for the whole bundle, the same figure used for a single 4-pair cable on a horizontal run","The tension rating the manufacturer gives for that cable"],c:3,e:"The limit comes from the manufacturer of the cable being pulled. The 25 lbf figure belongs to a single 4-pair cable, and a winch is rated far above what a cable can take."},

// u4l8
{id:"u4l8-1",t:"u4l8",q:"An outdoor cable is to enter the building through the entrance facility. What is the rule for the cable inside?",a:["It must change to indoor-rated cable within the distance the NEC allows, usually at the entrance facility","It may run anywhere in the building if it is pulled through metal conduit","It may keep its outdoor jacket for the whole run when the AHJ is not on site","It must be limited to the first floor and labeled with the date of installation"],c:0,e:"Outdoor cable may enter only a short distance before it must be replaced or spliced to cable with an indoor fire rating. The entrance facility is where that change is made."},
{id:"u4l8-2",t:"u4l8",q:"The pull plan sends you into a manhole to set up the pulling eye. What must be true before you go in?",a:["The cover is off, the foreman has looked down it, and you carry a flashlight and a radio","It has been tested, ventilated, and permitted, with a standby person outside","The run is under 30 m (100 ft) and another trade has already been inside it once today","You wear a hard hat and gloves and tell the crew above that you will be down only a short time"],c:1,e:"A manhole is a confined space: it must be tested, ventilated, and permitted, with a standby person outside. An open cover, a flashlight, a short run, or PPE alone does not make it safe."},
{id:"u4l8-3",t:"u4l8",q:"You are to pull fiber through an underground duct bank with no innerduct. What is the BEST method?",a:["Pull the fiber through the open duct and tape it to the walls at each manhole","Pull the fiber with the largest rope available and hope that it clears the joints","Pull innerduct first, then run the cable inside it, leaving a labeled string","Pull the fiber together with the power conductors in the same duct"],c:2,e:"Innerduct gives the fiber a smooth, protected path and keeps a string in place. A bare duct, a heavy rope, or sharing with power puts the fiber at risk."},
{id:"u4l8-4",t:"u4l8",q:"You must plan the pull of a cable that runs between two TRs on the same floor. What is the BEST plan?",a:["Treat it as horizontal cabling and hold it to the 25 lbf limit for a 4-pair cable","Treat it as entrance facility cabling and change it to indoor cable at the first wall","Treat it as work area cabling and leave 305 mm (12 in) of slack at each end","Treat it as backbone: use a pulling eye, a swivel, and the maker's tension limit"],c:3,e:"Cabling between two TRs is backbone, even on one floor, so it is pulled with a pulling eye, a swivel, and the manufacturer's tension limit. The 25 lbf figure and the 305 mm slack belong to horizontal 4-pair cable."},
{id:"u4l8-5",t:"u4l8",q:"A 150 ft backbone pull between two buildings has a feed point and a pull point. What is the BEST plan?",a:["Feed from the reel at one point and pull at the other with a swivel and a tension meter","Start at the middle of the run and pull outward in both directions to finish in half the time","Splice the cable at the pull point and finish the run in two lengths","Pull from the far end with a short rope and no tension monitoring, since 150 ft is a short run"],c:0,e:"Feed at one point and pull at the other with a swivel and a tension meter, then coil the slack at a large radius. A splice is not an option and unmonitored pulling risks damage."},

// u4l9
{id:"u4l9-1",t:"u4l9",q:"A helper is about to clamp the pulling grip onto the glass and buffer of a fiber cable. What is the BEST correction?",a:["Attach it to the outer jacket alone, using a wrapped cable tie","Attach it to the strength member, the aramid yarn or central member","Clip it to the buffer coating of each fiber with small clips","Keep it on the fibers but spread the clamp over the whole buffer"],c:1,e:"The strength member carries the pull. Pulling on the fibers, the buffer, or the jacket alone stretches the glass and breaks it."},
{id:"u4l9-2",t:"u4l9",q:"A fiber cable 0.5 in across is installed in a tray with no load on it. What is the smallest bend radius allowed?",a:["2.5 in","10 in","5 in","20 in"],c:2,e:"With no load, fiber needs 10 x OD: 10 x 0.5 = 5 in. The 10 in figure is the 20 x OD value for pulling tension."},
{id:"u4l9-3",t:"u4l9",q:"A long fiber pull must be staged on the floor before it goes in. What is the BEST way to lay out the cable?",a:["Coil it in one big circle so that it can come out from the middle","Spread it flat in a long line along the corridor wall","Stack it in a tight pile near the conduit mouth","Lay it in a figure eight, and flip it as you feed"],c:3,e:"A figure eight puts opposite twists in the two lobes, so they cancel. A single circle twists the cable once per loop."},
{id:"u4l9-4",t:"u4l9",q:"A fiber cable has been pulled and its ends will sit in the ceiling for two days before termination. What is the BEST protection?",a:["Cap the ends and keep them up off the floor","Wrap the ends tightly with electrical tape and lay them flat on the nearest ceiling tile","Leave the ends bare so that moisture on the glass can dry off in the ceiling air","Tie the ends to the nearest J-hook with a knot so that they cannot be pulled back"],c:0,e:"Caps or a pulling sleeve keep dirt and moisture off the ends. Bare ends, tape, or a knot invite contamination or damage."},
{id:"u4l9-5",t:"u4l9",q:"A fiber cable's data sheet lists a maximum pulling tension, and a helper says to use 25 lbf because that is the limit for every cable. What do you do?",a:["Use 25 lbf, since that is the standard limit for every cable","Stay below the maximum on the cable's specification sheet","Use the rating of the pulling grip as the limit","Pull until it feels tight and then back off slightly"],c:1,e:"The manufacturer's rating for that cable is the limit. The 25 lbf figure belongs to 4-pair copper."},

// u4l10
{id:"u4l10-1",t:"u4l10",q:"The foreman checks your outlet after termination and asks how much slack you left on the 4-pair cable. What is the least slack that is acceptable?",a:["152 mm (6 in)","610 mm (24 in)","305 mm (12 in)","1 m (3.3 ft)"],c:2,e:"At least 305 mm (12 in) remains after termination, and it is not stored in loops. Six inches is too little for a retermination."},
{id:"u4l10-2",t:"u4l10",q:"You are cabling a TE for a small space on a floor, with the cable pulled from the TR. The foreman asks how much slack to store outside the enclosure. What is the figure?",a:["1 m (3.3 ft)","305 mm (12 in)","5 m (16 ft)","2 m (6.6 ft)"],c:3,e:"The slack outside a TE is 2 m (6.6 ft), enough for reterminations and moves."},
{id:"u4l10-3",t:"u4l10",q:"A multipair backbone cable lands in a TR. How much slack should be left there?",a:["The length of the backboard plus floor to ceiling","Enough to reach the nearest wall only","Exactly 305 mm (12 in)","None, since multipair cable is never reterminated"],c:0,e:"Multipair slack in a TR is the length of the backboard plus the run from floor to ceiling. This allows reterminating later."},
{id:"u4l10-4",t:"u4l10",q:"The pulled cables have to be dressed into the rack. What is the BEST method?",a:["Bundle every cable into one large group with plastic ties pulled tight","Group them by patch panel, in port order, with hook-and-loop ties, snug but loose","Leave the cables loose and hanging so that nobody has to untie them","Tie them with wire to the rack's frame for strength"],c:1,e:"Cables dressed in port order with hook-and-loop ties land neatly and are easy to service. Tight ties crush, and loose cables tangle."},
{id:"u4l10-5",t:"u4l10",q:"A service loop is to be stored in the ceiling. What is the BEST way to lay it?",a:["In a tight twist tied at its middle with a cable tie so that it takes little room above the tile","Pushed up behind a ceiling tile and left unsupported","In a coil larger than the cable's bend radius, supported on a hook or rack","Hung from the nearest grid wire in a figure eight, with the weight resting on one wire"],c:2,e:"A loop must be larger than the cable's minimum bend radius and supported so that its weight does not hang on one point."},

// u5l1
{id:"u5l1-1",t:"u5l1",q:"A helper fans out the pairs on a Category 6 jack and untwists each one about an inch to dress them neatly. What is the most untwist you allow?",a:["50 mm (2 in)","25 mm (1 in)","6 mm (0.25 in)","13 mm (0.5 in)"],c:3,e:"The limit for Category 5e and above is 13 mm (0.5 in). Every extra millimeter of untwist picks up and leaks crosstalk, so an inch of it will fail the link. The 6 mm and 25 mm values are not the rule."},
{id:"u5l1-2",t:"u5l1",q:"While opening a cable at the rack, the jacket tool nicks the insulation of one conductor. What is the BEST action?",a:["Cut the cable back past the damage, restrip it, and start the termination over","Turn the nicked conductor toward the back of the jack so that the damage is out of sight","Wrap the nick in vinyl tape and punch it down, since the wiremap will still pass","Punch the nicked conductor down last so any weakness affects less of the link"],c:0,e:"A nick is a future failure: heat and flexing break the copper, and the link goes open months later. Cut back past the damage and restrip. Tape or hiding the nick leaves the weak conductor in the link."},
{id:"u5l1-3",t:"u5l1",q:"A helper strips a quarter inch of insulation from every conductor before punching down on a 110 block. What do you tell him?",a:["That is correct, because the blades need bare copper to make contact with the conductor","Stop; the insulation stays on because the IDC cuts through it as the wire seats","Strip a half inch instead, so the blades have more bare copper","Strip only the striped conductors, which have thicker insulation"],c:1,e:"An insulation displacement connector cuts through the insulation as the wire seats and grips the copper itself. Stripping loosens that grip and invites corrosion."},
{id:"u5l1-4",t:"u5l1",q:"The cable you are about to open has a rip cord under the jacket. What is the BEST way to use it?",a:["Cut the cord off flush with the jacket so it cannot catch in the jack when you terminate","Pull the cord out of the cable with pliers before you score the jacket, then strip normally","Score the jacket lightly, pull the cord down, and cut the jacket away","Wrap the cord around the conductors behind the jack as an extra strain relief for the cable"],c:2,e:"The rip cord opens the jacket cleanly without a blade touching the pairs. It is not a strain relief, and cutting it off or pulling it out first wastes it."},
{id:"u5l1-5",t:"u5l1",q:"The label on the cable at the TR reads 2A-021, but the pull schedule and the port label call for 2A-012. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Terminate it on port 12, since the numbers are only two digits swapped","Relabel it 2A-012 with a printed label and carry on","Terminate it on port 21 and let the tester settle which is right","Check the schedule and the label at the outlet end to learn which one is wrong"],c:3,e:"The label, schedule, and port label must agree before you terminate. Checking the other end finds the real cable. Relabeling or guessing can put a cable on the wrong port and ruin the records."},

// u5l2
{id:"u5l2-1",t:"u5l2",q:"A Category 6A link certifies only at Category 5e limits. The cable and the patch panel are both Category 6A. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["A Category 5e jack somewhere in the permanent link","A pull tension of 20 lbf, which is under the 25 lbf limit","A service loop coiled above the TR ladder rack","Slack at the outlet cut back to 305 mm (12 in)"],c:0,e:"The weakest part sets the category of the whole link, so one Category 5e jack makes the link a Category 5e link. The 20 lbf pull, the loop, and the 305 mm slack are all acceptable."},
{id:"u5l2-2",t:"u5l2",q:"The drawing calls for a Category 6 cross-connect in the TR, and the only blocks on the shelf are 66 blocks. What should you do?",a:["Use the 66 blocks, since the clips carry voice and data alike on Category 6 cable","Get 110 blocks, because a 66 block is for voice only and not for high-speed data","Use the 66 blocks and note the lower speed on the drawings for the next crew","Use the 66 blocks and rely on short jumpers to keep the data signal clean"],c:1,e:"A 66 block is a voice-grade termination and is not for high-speed data. A Category 6 cross-connect needs a 110 block, and a note on the drawings does not make the 66 block acceptable."},
{id:"u5l2-3",t:"u5l2",q:"You are seating wire on a 110 block with a punch tool. How should the blade face?",a:["Either way, because the tool seats and trims in both directions","Cutting side in, toward the cable you are keeping","Cutting side out, toward the end of the wire to be trimmed","Sideways, along the row of slots, so the cut is clean"],c:2,e:"The cutting side goes out so the tool trims the excess as it seats the wire. Facing in cuts the length you need or leaves the extra uncut."},
{id:"u5l2-4",t:"u5l2",q:"A fiber panel is fitted with LC adapters, and the pigtails delivered have SC connectors. What is the BEST action?",a:["Seat the SC connectors in the LC adapters and accept the small loss","Cut the SC connectors off and tape the bare fibers into the adapters","Mate them with a short APC patch cord to cover the difference","Fit SC adapters, or order LC pigtails, so the connectors match the panel"],c:3,e:"Adapters and connectors must be the same type. Forcing, taping, or mixing end faces damages the fiber and loses signal. Fit matching adapters or get matching pigtails."},
{id:"u5l2-5",t:"u5l2",q:"A new horizontal cable has been pulled into the TR. Where does it end?",a:["On a patch panel or a 110 block, in the rack or on the wall","On a keystone jack bolted to the backboard of the telecommunications room","On a 66 block in the entrance facility, before the outside protectors","On a fiber cassette"],c:0,e:"Horizontal cable ends in the TR on a patch panel or a 110 block. The keystone jack is the work area end."},

// u5l3
{id:"u5l3-1",t:"u5l3",q:"The job specification says T568B. At the last jack of the day the helper reaches for the T568A color code printed on the housing. What do you tell him?",a:["Either label works as long as the jack is a good one","Use T568B, the same scheme as every other jack, panel, and cord on the job","T568A is better for data, so use it from here on","Use T568A on the left of the faceplate and T568B on the right"],c:1,e:"One scheme per job. Mixing schemes crosses the orange and green pairs and the tester shows a wiremap fault."},
{id:"u5l3-2",t:"u5l3",q:"The job uses T568B. Which conductor lands on pin 6?",a:["Orange","White-green","Green","White-orange"],c:2,e:"In T568B pin 6 is green and pin 3 is white-green. In T568A pin 6 is orange. Pins 1 and 2 are white-orange and orange in T568B."},
{id:"u5l3-3",t:"u5l3",q:"The tester shows pairs 2 and 3 crossed on one link, with no opens or shorts. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["A patch cord with a damaged plug at the tester end","A staple driven through the cable that shorts two pairs","A link longer than the 90 m permanent link limit","One end of the link was terminated T568A and the other end T568B"],c:3,e:"T568A and T568B swap the orange and green pairs. If the two ends use different schemes, those two pairs cross."},
{id:"u5l3-4",t:"u5l3",q:"A helper is converting a jack from T568A to T568B and asks which pair on pins 4 and 5 has to move. Which pair is on those pins?",a:["Blue","Green","Orange","Brown"],c:0,e:"The blue pair is on pins 4 and 5 in both schemes, so it does not move. Only the orange and green pairs swap."},
{id:"u5l3-5",t:"u5l3",q:"The job uses T568B. A helper crimps a field cord with T568B at one end and T568A at the other, and the foreman asked for a standard cord. What is the BEST action?",a:["Keep it as a crossover and label it for use between two switches in the rack","Recrimp the T568A end in T568B so the cord is straight-through","Keep it, since modern equipment senses the swap and the tester will pass it","Recrimp both ends in T568A, the older scheme, so the cord matches itself"],c:1,e:"A standard patch cord is straight-through with the job's one scheme at both ends. A mixed cord is a crossover, which is not what was asked for, and the job's scheme is T568B."},

// u5l4
{id:"u5l4-1",t:"u5l4",q:"On a 25-pair backbone cable you must punch down the pair colored red-orange. What pair number is it?",a:["6","12","7","17"],c:2,e:"Red is tip group 2 and orange is ring position 2, so n = 5 x (2 - 1) + 2 = 7."},
{id:"u5l4-2",t:"u5l4",q:"A tester shows that pair 19 of a 25-pair cable is open. Which colors do you look for?",a:["Violet-brown","Yellow-green","Black-brown","Yellow-brown"],c:3,e:"For pair 19, 19 - 1 = 18. Dividing by 5 gives 3 remainder 3, so the tip is the fourth color (yellow) and the ring is the fourth color (brown)."},
{id:"u5l4-3",t:"u5l4",q:"In a 150-pair cable you need pair 60. What color is the binder group that holds it?",a:["White-green","White-blue","White-orange","White-brown"],c:0,e:"Binder 1 holds pairs 1 to 25, binder 2 holds 26 to 50, and binder 3 holds 51 to 75. Binder colors follow the pair code, so binder 3 is white-green."},
{id:"u5l4-4",t:"u5l4",q:"A 100-pair cable has been pulled to the TR and the block is ready. What is the BEST order for the termination?",a:["Start with the last binder and work back toward the first","Work binder by binder and pair by pair in color order from pair 1","Terminate the easy pairs first and fill the others in as time allows","Terminate in whatever order the pairs come out of the stripped jacket"],c:1,e:"Terminating in color order makes a wrong pair show up as a break in the pattern. Out-of-order work crosses pairs and mislabels the block."},
{id:"u5l4-5",t:"u5l4",q:"A helper wants to fit bridging clips to a Category 6 data cross-connect on a 66 block. What is the BEST response?",a:["Fit them, since the clips are rated for any application on the block","Fit them only on the white-blue pairs, which carry the least traffic","Do not; the clips are for voice cross-connects and are not rated for category performance","Fit them but only on the last row of the block, away from the cable entry"],c:2,e:"Bridging clips make and open voice circuits for testing and are not rated for category performance. Data cross-connects use hardware rated for the category."},

// u5l5
{id:"u5l5-1",t:"u5l5",q:"A crew is wiring new cable television drops in a house. Which coax is the standard choice for new work?",a:["RG-58","RG-59","RG-11","RG-6"],c:3,e:"RG-6 is the 75 ohm standard for television and video in new work. RG-59 is thinner with more loss, and RG-11 is for long feeders."},
{id:"u5l5-2",t:"u5l5",q:"A crew has been installing twist-on F connectors and the drops are failing within weeks. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Replace them with compression connectors installed with the right tool","Tighten each twist-on connector with pliers so that it cannot work loose","Wrap each connector in electrical tape to stop movement at the fitting","Shorten each drop so the connector sits closer to the wall plate"],c:0,e:"Twist-on connectors loosen and can let braid touch the center conductor. A compression connector grips the cable evenly and holds."},
{id:"u5l5-3",t:"u5l5",q:"A new camera's picture drops out. At the connector you find stray braid strands touching the center conductor. What is the MOST likely fault?",a:["A bad ground at the monitor that lets the signal leak away","A short between the center conductor and the braid shield at the connector","An open center conductor where the pin was cut off inside the fitting","Excess loss, because RG-6 is the wrong cable for a camera run"],c:1,e:"Braid touching the center conductor shorts the signal. Remove the connector, trim the strands, and reterminate."},
{id:"u5l5-4",t:"u5l5",q:"A helper is about to fit an F connector on coax that has a stranded center conductor. What is the correct response?",a:["Solder the strands into a ball so that they fill the connector's pin socket","Go ahead, since the strands will form a pin when the connector is compressed","Stop; an F connector uses the cable's own solid center conductor as its pin","Strip a longer center conductor so the strands bend back over the dielectric"],c:2,e:"An F connector has no center pin and relies on a solid center conductor that is strong enough to be the pin. Stranded wire will not hold."},
{id:"u5l5-5",t:"u5l5",q:"A security camera's coax needs a connector at the recorder. Which is the usual choice?",a:["F, the threaded connector used for cable television","A twist-on F connector, favored because it fits without a tool","A crimp-style F connector, chosen for high-bandwidth video","BNC, a bayonet connector used for security video"],c:3,e:"BNC is the usual connector for security video and test equipment. The F connector is for cable television, and twist-on connectors are strongly discouraged."},

// u5l6
{id:"u5l6-1",t:"u5l6",q:"A patch cord with a green connector body is about to go into a blue adapter on a panel. What should you do?",a:["Stop; an APC end face must not mate with a UPC, so get matching types","Plug it in, because the small loss is acceptable on a short patch","Plug it in and clean the end face afterwards to restore the polish","Turn the connector to a different angle until the ferrules seat"],c:0,e:"Green is APC and blue is UPC. The angled and flat faces damage each other and lose signal, so match the types."},
{id:"u5l6-2",t:"u5l6",q:"A 24-fiber backbone must be terminated with the lowest loss possible. Which method is the BEST?",a:["Field-polished connectors, polished by hand at the panel","Fusion-spliced pigtails, spliced on a splice tray in the panel","Mechanical splice connectors, joined with index-matching gel","Crimped connectors, squeezed onto the buffer with a tool"],c:1,e:"A fusion splice joins a factory pigtail to the fiber with the lowest loss. Field polish and mechanical splices lose more, and a crimp is not a fiber termination method."},
{id:"u5l6-3",t:"u5l6",q:"The adapters in a fiber panel are green, and a helper holds one cord with a blue connector body and one with a green body. Which cord is the BEST choice?",a:["Either one, because the adapter sleeve lines the two faces up whatever the polish","The blue cord, because blue marks the lower-loss angled face","The green cord, because green marks an angled APC face that mates only with another APC","Neither, because green adapters take only multifiber MPO connectors"],c:2,e:"Green is APC and blue is UPC. The angled and flat faces damage each other and lose signal, so match the types."},
{id:"u5l6-4",t:"u5l6",q:"You cut a fiber and a tiny shard drops on the bench. What is the BEST way to handle it?",a:["Brush it onto the floor and sweep the area at the end of the shift","Drop it in the nearest open trash can along with the wipes and wrappers","Blow it off the bench","Put it in a sealed disposal container kept at the bench"],c:3,e:"Fiber shards are sharp and nearly invisible. Use a sealed disposal container and keep food and drink away from the work area."},
{id:"u5l6-5",t:"u5l6",q:"A new 120 m duplex multimode link shows no light at the receiver. Both end faces are clean and the labels look right. What is the MOST likely fault?",a:["Polarity is reversed, so transmit lands on transmit","The fiber is bent at 10 x its OD with no load, too wide for the light to stay in the core","The cable has a 900 micron tight buffer, which blocks light unless it is loose tube","The cladding is 125 micron, which is too large for a link of this length"],c:0,e:"Transmit at one end must land on receive at the other, and a polarity error stops the link until the pair is crossed. Tight buffer is normal indoors, 10 x OD is the correct no-load radius, and 125 micron is the standard cladding."},

// u5l7
{id:"u5l7-1",t:"u5l7",q:"A fiber link tests high in loss, and the connectors look clean to the eye. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Replace the cable, since high loss means the glass has broken","Inspect each end face with a scope and clean any contamination you find","Fusion-splice the fiber again at the panel to remove the loss","Retest with a shorter launch cord and accept the new reading"],c:1,e:"Contamination is the most common fiber fault and cannot be seen without a scope. Inspect, clean, and inspect again before you touch the cable."},
{id:"u5l7-2",t:"u5l7",q:"A dust cap falls on the floor, and you pick it up. What should you do?",a:["Put it back on the connector, because a cap only needs to keep out the larger dust","Blow the dust off the cap and snap it on, since the connector face is covered","Clean the connector before mating it and replace the dirty cap","Rub it on your sleeve"],c:2,e:"A cap that touched the floor is dirty and can carry that dirt onto the end face. Clean the connector before you mate it."},
{id:"u5l7-3",t:"u5l7",q:"A connector end face is dirty. What is the BEST first cleaning method?",a:["Soak a wipe in solvent and scrub the end face hard until the dust is gone","Rub the face on a clean cotton shirt sleeve with a firm circular motion","Blow across the end face with a short breath of air to lift the dust away","Dry-clean it with a one-click cleaner or a cassette cleaner"],c:3,e:"Dry cleaning comes first. Wet-to-dry is used only if dry cleaning fails, and breath, cloth, and heavy scrubbing leave oil or scratch the face."},
{id:"u5l7-4",t:"u5l7",q:"You want to look at a connector on a link that may be live. What is the safest practice?",a:["Never look into a fiber end with a source on; use a scope with a filter","Look through a hand lens, since glass blocks the invisible light","Look at it for only a second or two, because a brief glance cannot harm the eye","Hold it up to a work lamp and look at the face by eye"],c:0,e:"Never look into a fiber end with a source on. Treat every fiber as live until it is proven dark, and inspect with a scope that has a filter."},
{id:"u5l7-5",t:"u5l7",q:"After cleaning an end face, the scope still shows a scratch across the core. What is the BEST action?",a:["Clean it once more with a wet-to-dry wipe until the scratch is gone from the core","Replace the connector; a scratch is damage","Mate it anyway and test the link, since a scratch is only cosmetic","Polish the face with a dry swab until the scratch fades"],c:1,e:"A scratch is damage that cleaning cannot remove, and a scratched core loses light. The connector must be replaced."},

// u5l8
{id:"u5l8-1",t:"u5l8",q:"A shielded panel shows six ports with open shields, and the helper cut the drain wires to make the termination neat. What is the BEST fix?",a:["Leave them as they are, because the wiremap passes on every port","Ground the open shield at the far end only, with a short jumper","Reterminate those cables with the drain wire and foil on the shield contact","Tape the cut drain wire back along the jacket and carry on"],c:2,e:"The shield must be continuous and terminated. A cut drain wire leaves an open shield that acts as an antenna, and a passing wiremap says nothing about the shield."},
{id:"u5l8-2",t:"u5l8",q:"A shielded cable is to be terminated on a jack. What type of jack is correct?",a:["Any jack will do, because the shield is part of the cable and carries on by itself","A plastic jack, to keep the shield isolated","A jack with a longer faceplate","A shielded jack whose metal body contacts the cable's shield"],c:3,e:"A shielded cable in an unshielded jack is an unshielded cable. The jack's metal body must contact the shield."},
{id:"u5l8-3",t:"u5l8",q:"Three shielded racks sit in a row. How should they be bonded?",a:["Give each rack its own conductor back to the busbar","Bond only the first rack, because the racks are bolted to the same floor slab","Bond rack 1 to the busbar, then rack 2 to rack 1 and rack 3 to rack 2 in a chain","Rely on each rack's power cord ground as the bond, since it is already a green wire"],c:0,e:"Never daisy-chain. Each rack gets its own conductor back to the busbar, and a power cord ground is never the only ground for a rack."},
{id:"u5l8-4",t:"u5l8",q:"The tester shows a shield open at one outlet in a shielded system, while every pair passes the wiremap. The foreman wants to leave it. What is the MOST likely result?",a:["Nothing, because the pairs inside still twist and perform like a good shielded link","The open shield can act as an antenna, picking up noise and radiating it","The link gains a better ground, so crosstalk improves","The cable reads shorter by the length of the break"],c:1,e:"A broken or poorly bonded shield can pick up and radiate noise, so it can be worse than no shield at all. A passing wiremap says nothing about the shield."},
{id:"u5l8-5",t:"u5l8",q:"After the rack is bonded, the electrician runs a two-point bonding test and the foreman asks what reading you need to see. What is the recommended maximum resistance?",a:["0.5 ohm (500 milliohms)","1 ohm (1000 milliohms)","0.1 ohm (100 milliohms)","10 ohms (10000 milliohms)"],c:2,e:"The recommended maximum is 0.1 ohm (100 milliohms). Higher readings mean a poor connection to the busbar and need to be fixed."}
);
